<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/040P">Remark <span data-tag="040P">8.5.4.21</span>.</a> Broadly speaking, Proposition <a data-tag="040M" href="/tag/040M">8.5.4.19</a> will be useful to us because it shows that the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$ admits a (left and right) cofinal diagram $Q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$, where the simplicial set $K$ is finite-dimensional. Beware that it is not possible to arrange that the simplicial set $K$ is <em>finite</em>, since an $\infty $-category which admits finite colimits need not be idempotent-complete (Warning <a data-tag="0408" href="/tag/0408">8.5.4.4</a>). In particular, there does not exist a categorical equivalence $K \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{Idem})$, where $K$ is a finite simplicial set. </p>
